<!-- Declares the WiFi HAL.

     NO <version> ELEMENT, ON PURPOSE. Do not "fix" this by adding one.

     This file used to declare <version>4</version>, justified as "read off the
     binary, not assumed: android.hardware.wifi-service links
     android.hardware.wifi-V4-ndk.so". That reasoning was circular: the binary
     only linked V4 because our own extract-files.py had rewritten its DT_NEEDED
     from V1 to V4. Motorola built it against V1.

     The consequence was not a registration failure but something much harder to
     read. IWifi registered fine, then the HAL SIGABRTed the instant the
     framework called into IWifiStaIface, inside the V4 NDK backend's
     onTransact, free()ing a pointer of 0x3. The relink has been removed and
     common.mk now ships android.hardware.wifi-V1-ndk.vendor instead.

     Omitting <version> is what stock does. Its
     vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/android.hardware.wifi-service.xml carries only a
     <name> and an <fqname>.

     Do not take "stock does it" as the justification, though, because it does not
     transfer: stock's ro.board.api_level selects FCM 202404, which asks for wifi
     1-2, and a version-less AIDL entry defaults to 1. This build is 202504, whose
     matrix asks 2-3, so 1 is outside it.

     The declaration is nevertheless harmless, for reasons worth writing down:
     libvintf in this tree does not version-check HALs at all (HalManifest::
     checkCompatibility validates schema type, VNDK, SDK, sepolicy and kernel and
     has no HAL loop), check_vintf is not part of the build target used here, and
     servicemanager's isDeclared matches package/interface/instance without a
     version. So 4, 1 and absent behave identically, and the version that actually
     governs runtime is the one the implementation reports from
     getInterfaceVersion(), i.e. 1, because that is what the blob was compiled
     against.

     The real cost is not this file: running a V1 implementation means anything
     the Android 16 wifi framework gates on version >= 2 (MLO / Wi-Fi 7, the newer
     TWT and channel APIs) is unavailable, and an unguarded newer call would come
     back UNKNOWN_TRANSACTION. That is inherent to the blob, not to the manifest.

     Verified live on the device before this file was written: IWifi/default and
     ISupplicant/default both register, wpa_supplicant runs, wlan0 comes UP as
     ROLE_CLIENT_PRIMARY, and a scan returns networks on both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz.

     Filename deliberately differs from any AOSP-shipped fragment name so kati
     cannot fail with "already defined by", the same reason
     android.hardware.sensors-arcfox.xml is named as it is. -->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device">
    <hal format="aidl">
        <name>android.hardware.wifi</name>
        <fqname>IWifi/default</fqname>
    </hal>
</manifest>
